#! mathcas 2 notebook

#% title
# inverse_weierstrass_p

#% text
# `inverse_weierstrass_p(p, [g2, g3])` gives a `u` with `weierstrass_p(u, [g2, g3]) = p`.

#% section
# Details

#% item
# `u` is plus or minus `R_F(p - e1, p - e2, p - e3)` (DLMF 23.6.31 with the roots `e1`, `e2`, `e3` of `4 t^3 - g2 t - g3`), the sign the one with `weierstrass_p_prime(u) = sqrt(4 p^3 - g2 p - g3)` (the principal root); for real arguments a negative real `u` is moved by the real period.

#% item
# `inverse_weierstrass_p([p, q], [g2, g3])` takes the sign with `weierstrass_p_prime(u, [g2, g3]) = q`.

#% item
# The other solutions differ from `u` by a sign and a period.

#% section
# Examples

#% subsection
# Basic examples

#% cell
inverse_weierstrass_p(7.0, [1, 2])
#% output
#= 2.2385322312096347

#% cell
n(inverse_weierstrass_p(1/3, [1, 2]), 30)
#% output
#= -1.3083647626370968653240501853341 + 0.5836990519927154443754597137060im

#% cell
inverse_weierstrass_p(3.0 + im, [im, im + 2])
#% output
#= -0.5566694624256354 + 0.08952411123355775im

#% cell
u = inverse_weierstrass_p(2.5, [1, 2]); weierstrass_p(u, [1, 2])
#% output
#= 2.5000000000000004

#% cell
inverse_weierstrass_p([7.0, -sqrt(4*343.0 - 9)], [1, 2])
#% output
#= 0.37819729406455915

